     "text": "```python\ndef least_common_multiple(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the least common multiple (LCM) of two integers.\n    \n    The LCM of two integers is the smallest positive integer that is \n    divisible by both numbers. This function uses the mathematical \n    relationship between the greatest common divisor (GCD) and LCM.\n    \n    Args:\n        a (int): First integer.\n        b (int): Second integer.\n    \n    Returns:\n        int: The least common multiple of a and b.\n    \"\"\"\n    def greatest_common_divisor(x: int, y: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculate the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers.\n        \n        Args:\n            x (int): First integer.\n            y (int): Second integer.\n        \n        Returns:\n            int: The greatest common divisor of x and y.\n        \"\"\"\n        while y:\n            x, y = y, x % y\n        return x\n\n    if a < 0 or b < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Both integers must be non-negative.\")\n\n    return abs(a * b) // greatest_common_divisor(a, b)\n```",
